canonical_claim.statement:
Corrected statement. Let $n\ge1$. Replace Assumption 1 by: Assumption 1' (unbiasedness at the treated evaluation observations): for every policy $\pi$ in the feasible set and every unit $i$, $E[T_i^\pi(\hat\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i))\mid X_i]=0$; equivalently, $E[\hat\mu_0(X_i)\mid X_i,T_i^\pi=1]=\mu_0(X_i)$ on the event $\{\pi(X_i)>0\}$. The estimator $\hat\mu_0$ may be random, and the expectation is taken over it as well. All displayed estimator evaluations are measurable, and Assumption 1' requires $E[|T_i^\pi(\hat\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i))|]<\infty$ for every feasible fixed policy and unit, so its conditional-expectation equality has its ordinary integrable meaning. Under the standing assumptions (S1)-(S3) and (S9) and Assumption 1':
(i) (the identity of Proposition 9) for every $\pi$ in the feasible set, $E[w_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=nE[\pi(X_i)\tau(X_i)]=nV(\pi)$;
(ii) (Proposition 3) for every feasible set $\Pi$, $\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}E[w_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)$, so every best response $\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}}$ has $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}})=0$, 'regardless of the feasible set $\Pi$';
(iii) (the rule of Proposition 9) if $\Pi$ is the set of all functions $\pi:\mathcal X\to[0,1]$, then $\pi(x)=\mathbb 1(\tau(x)>0)$ is a best response to $w_{\mathrm{TT}}$;
(iv) (a sufficient implementation) if the estimator $\hat\mu_0$, as a jointly measurable random function on $\mathcal X$, is independent of $(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi)$ and $E[\hat\mu_0(x)]=\mu_0(x)$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$ (for example an estimator computed from auxiliary data independent of the treatment population and of the treatment randomization), and $E[|T_i^\pi(\hat\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i))|]<\infty$ for every feasible fixed $\pi$ and every unit $i$, then Assumption 1' holds for every such $\pi$.

Printed. PDF p. 6: 'Theorem 1 (Incentive Alignment). Suppose $\hat\mu_0(x)$ is unbiased, and $\pi$ is unconstrained. For any function $g:\mathcal X\to\mathbb R^+$, $w^g_{TT}$ yields an agent best response with regret $R(\pi^{w^g_{TT}})=0$.' PDF p. 19: 'Theorem 1 (Incentive Alignment). Suppose $E[\hat\mu_0(x)]=\mu_0(x)$, and suppose $\Pi$ is the set of all functions $\pi:\mathcal X\to[0,1]$. For any function $g:\mathcal X\to\mathbb R^+$, $w^g_{TT}$ yields an agent best response with zero regret.' The proof (p. 19) uses 'Since $E[\hat\mu_0(x)]=\mu_0(x)$ and we have ignorability in $X_i$, $E[Y_i-\hat\mu_0(X_i)|T_i^\pi=1,X_i]=\tau(X_i)$', obtains '$E[w^g_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf Y,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf X)]=nE[g(X_i)\tau(X_i)\pi(X_i)]$', and ends 'Since $g(X_i)>0$, the treatment rule $\pi$ that maximizes this is the same as $\pi^*$ from Lemma 1. Therefore, the regret is zero.'

Corrected statement. Let $\Pi$ be the set of all (measurable) functions $\pi:\mathcal X\to[0,1]$, let $g:\mathcal X\to(0,\infty)$ be a fixed nonrandom measurable function, and suppose the standing assumptions (S1)-(S3), (S9) and Assumption 1' of target P006-T6 hold for every $\pi\in\Pi$ ($E[T_i^\pi(\hat\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i))\mid X_i]=0$), with integrable potential outcomes and treated estimator residuals $T_i^\pi(\hat\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i))$, integrable $g\tau$, and integrable individual weighted reward summands $(Y_i-\hat\mu_0(X_i))T_i^\pi g(X_i)$, for every policy and unit; integrability of $\hat\mu_0(X_i)$ on untreated observations is not required. Then:
(i) for every $\pi\in\Pi$, $E[w^g_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=nE[g(X_i)\tau(X_i)\pi(X_i)]$;
(ii) $\pi^*(x)=\mathbb 1(\tau(x)>0)$ maximizes $E[w^g_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]$ over $\Pi$;
(iii) every maximizer $\pi'$ of $E[w^g_{\mathrm{TT}}]$ over $\Pi$ satisfies $\pi'(X_i)=1$ almost surely on $\{\tau(X_i)>0\}$ and $\pi'(X_i)=0$ almost surely on $\{\tau(X_i)<0\}$, so $V(\pi')=E[\tau(X_i)\mathbb 1(\tau(X_i)>0)]=\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)$ and $R(\pi^{w^g_{TT}})=0$ for every best response.

Printed. PDF p. 7: 'Theorem 3 (Regret With Information Asymmetry). Suppose $\hat\mu_0(x)$ is unbiased. Under Assumption 2, the regret is upper bounded as $R(\pi^{w_{TT}})\leq2\gamma_{marg}$.' PDF p. 22: 'Theorem 3 (Regret With Information Asymmetry). If the principal applies the reward function from $w_{TT}$ with an unbiased estimate $\hat\mu_0(X)$ where $E[\hat\mu_0(x)]=\mu_0(x)$, then under Assumption 2, the regret is upper bounded as $R(\pi^{w_{TT}})\leq2\gamma_{marg}$.' The proof (p. 22) uses 'By the tower property, $E[\pi(X,U)\hat\mu_0(X)]=E[E[\hat\mu_0(X_i)\pi(X_i,U_i)|X_i,U_i]]=E[\pi(X_i,U_i)E[\hat\mu_0(X_i)|X_i,U_i]]=E[\pi(X_i,U_i)\mu_0(X_i)]$.'

Corrected statement. Setting (S8) with (S9): the agent observes $U_i$ and chooses $\pi:\mathcal X\times\mathcal U\to[0,1]$ from an arbitrary feasible set $\Pi$; $P(T_i^\pi=1|X_i,U_i)=\pi(X_i,U_i)$; the treatment indicator is independent of the potential outcomes given $(X_i,U_i)$; $V(\pi)=E[\pi(X_i,U_i)\tau(X_i,U_i)]$; $\mu_0(X_i)=E[\mu_0(X_i,U_i)|X_i]$. Assume, in place of Assumption 1: for every $\pi\in\Pi$ and every unit $i$, $E[T_i^\pi(\hat\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i))\mid X_i,U_i]=0$ (equivalently $E[\hat\mu_0(X_i)\mid X_i,U_i,T_i^\pi=1]=\mu_0(X_i)$ on $\{\pi(X_i,U_i)>0\}$). This holds when $\hat\mu_0$ is independent of $(\mathbf X,\mathbf U,\mathbf T^\pi)$ and $E[\hat\mu_0(x)]=\mu_0(x)$ for all $x$, the auxiliary-data setting of Appendix D.2. Write $\hat V(\pi)=\frac1nE[w_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]$. Then:
(i) for every $\pi\in\Pi$, $\hat V(\pi)=E[\pi(X_i,U_i)(\mu_1(X_i,U_i)-\mu_0(X_i))]$;
(ii) for every $\pi\in\Pi$, $|\hat V(\pi)-V(\pi)|=|E[\pi(X_i,U_i)(\mu_0(X_i,U_i)-\mu_0(X_i))]|\leq E[|\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i,U_i)|]$;
(iii) under Assumption 2, for every $\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}}\in\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}E[w_{\mathrm{TT}}]$ and $\pi^*\in\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)$: $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}})=V(\pi^*)-V(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}})\leq2\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}$.
